plan Diagnostics
title   Diagnostics
status  archived
goal
    Define the canonical Flow State diagnostic value, typed failure
    classification/projection boundaries, usage/admission surface, and actionable
    remediation contract. This Plan owns migrated semantics under
    @plan.Verification.D1; ERRORS.ts is source provenance. These are target
    requirements, not implementation claims. Public route disposition belongs to
    @plan.PublicSurface.O1; product cutover remains pending under @plan.Cutover.O3.

outcomes
    O1  Canonical Schema-backed Diagnostic model
        - Diagnostic is the one package-owned diagnostic value. Its structured fields are the
          compatibility surface; human text can evolve. It is a Schema.TaggedError with tag
          flow-state/Diagnostic, a real Error, and a directly yieldable Effect failure. It is the
          executable Schema codec; there is no wrapper or second expected-error family.

        - DiagnosticPath and FlowPath have exact type (string | number)[]; FlowPath is an alias of
          DiagnosticPath. DiagnosticScalar is string | number | boolean | null. DiagnosticDetails is
          Record<string, DiagnosticScalar>. DiagnosticClassification is exactly Failure | Defect |
          Interruption. DiagnosticCode is the closed union below; summaries/help text do not add
          codes.

        - FlowUsageCode remains exactly these 19 values: InvalidCanonicalValue, ForeignActorRef,
          MismatchedActorRef, MissingActorRef, DisposedActorRef, RuntimeNotReady, RuntimeDisposed,
          MissingContextProvider, ContextDependencyCycle, DuplicateActorClaim, UnadmittedMachine,
          ActorNotActive, InvalidOperationPlan, WrongOperationKind, OperationNotPending,
          OperationAlreadySettled, DuplicateStreamDeclaration, BlockedByDependents,
          AdmissionOverloaded.

        - DiagnosticCode is FlowUsageCode plus exactly these closed codes:
          - InvalidMachineConfiguration, SchemaValidation, SemanticValidation, InvalidDescriptorId,
            DuplicateDescriptorId, DuplicateMachineValue, DuplicateModuleOwnership,
            MissingModuleReference, InvalidStateDefault, UnresolvedRequirementId.
          - InvalidStoryMetadata, InvalidTraceRecord, InvalidLifecycleTransition,
            InvalidStoryEvidence, InvalidArtifactOperand, WrongArtifactKind,
            ArtifactIdentityMismatch, DecompressionFailed, BoundExceeded, StorageFailure.
          - CodecFailure, IdentityVersionMismatch, MalformedData, ConcurrentCapture,
            NonDurableContextProvider, CleanupFailed, IoFailure, InvariantViolation, Defect,
            Interruption.

        - The Schema fields are classification, code, path, details, summary, and help.
        - classification is one of the three values above.
        - code is the closed DiagnosticCode literal set.
        - path is an array of strings or finite numbers.
        - details is a string-keyed record of strings, finite numbers, booleans, or null.
        - summary and help are strings.
          DiagnosticDocument is Schema.Codec.Encoded<typeof Diagnostic>.
          UsageDiagnostic narrows Diagnostic to classification Failure and code FlowUsageCode.
          DiagnosticProjection aliases Diagnostic; it is not another model.

        - InvalidMachineConfigurationReason is closed to exactly these values:
          - ConfigurationNotRecord, NonStringConfigurationKey, UnexpectedConfigurationField,
            StateConfigurationMismatch, ExpectedFunction, UnknownStateToken, UnknownEventToken,
            InvalidStateToken.
          - TransitionObjectRequired, ExpectedTransition, ExpectedEventHandlers,
            UnknownEventHandler, EmptyEventHandlerList, EmptyRedirectList, ExpectedRedirect,
            ExpectedTimer, InvalidTimerDelay.
          - ExpectedActivities, ExpectedStateConfiguration, MissingCompoundStateFields,
            InvalidDefaultTarget, ExpectedLeafConfiguration, InvalidCompoundState,
            InvalidTokenTables, AmbiguousHandler.
          Path/details identify field, token, or value without invented source lines.

        - This Plan owns the 19-code public usage shape and typed diagnostic model.
          ERRORS.ts is source provenance for declarations and implementation boundaries.
          AMEND-API-005 defers FlowPath, FlowUsageCode, and FlowUsageError from the
          active root until their owner and proofs exist.
          Their exact declarations remain future targets; source declarations do not restore active
          exports. Current route treatment belongs to @plan.PublicSurface.O1. Runtime read/error
          boundary terminology: @plan.Runtime.O5.

    O2  Structural, semantic, Effect, and Cause mapping
        - decodeStructure<S extends Schema.ConstraintDecoder<unknown, never>>(
          schema:S,input:unknown): Result.Result<S["Type"],Diagnostic>.
          It uses one private, service-free Effect Schema to decode untrusted structure.
          It returns plain data without running an Effect.
          It maps only SchemaError to Diagnostic with issue path and safe details.
          Machine compilation never decodes the already-decoded value again.

        - mapResultFailure<A,E>(result:Result.Result<A,E>,
          project:(failure:E)=>Diagnostic):Result.Result<A,Diagnostic> maps one semantic failure
          without throwing or widening A. Pure definition and machine authoring expose
          Result<A,Diagnostic>, including CompilationResult<A>.
          invalidMachineConfigurationDiagnostic(
          reason:InvalidMachineConfigurationReason,path:DiagnosticPath,
          details?:DiagnosticDetails):Diagnostic builds that same value.

        - mapEffectFailure<A,E,R>(effect:Effect.Effect<A,E,R>,
          project:(failure:E)=>Diagnostic):Effect.Effect<A,Diagnostic,R>.
          It translates one understood typed failure at its owner and preserves A and R exactly.
          DiagnosticEffect<A,R> is Effect.Effect<A,Diagnostic,R>.
          Failure values may be yielded directly or passed to Effect.fail.
          Defects and interruption remain in Cause until final classification.
          Name a foreign Promise adapter once. Use Effect.tryPromise({try,catch}).
          Its catch maps unknown once to an owned Diagnostic and forwards AbortSignal when
          supported.
          Do not use
          Effect.promise, nested runtime runners, or Promise rejection as a reusable failure
          channel.

        - Classification retains the complete Exit and Cause until cleanup and evidence aggregation
          finish. The private ClassifiedFailure<A,E> has classification, exit, cause, and
          Diagnostic[] diagnostics. classifyCause(exit:Exit.Exit<A,E>) remains package-private.
          Cause Fail, Die, and Interrupt remain distinguishable. Precedence is defect, then typed
          failure, then interruption-only; defect/interruption are not widened into E. Production
          completion uses Effect.exit, not Effect.result. Cause.squash is forbidden until an
          intentionally lossy synchronous JavaScript host boundary. Only FlowDisposeError and
          FlowStoryExecutionError may carry raw Cause, as @plan.PublicSurface.O1 specifies.
          Convenience JS throws/Promise rejections are terminal host projections, not reusable
          service channels.

        - renderDiagnostic(diagnostic):string is shared by diagnostic owners. Emit safe Rust-like
          text shaped as error[Code] at path: summary followed by actionable help: text. Escape
          path/details; do not invent file, line, or column. ANSI, stacks, raw input, and raw Cause
          are not renderer input. Formatting policy is separate from Schema/fields:
          DiagnosticPrinter has compact(diagnostic):string and pretty(diagnostic):string;
          DiagnosticPrinter, renderDiagnosticCompact, and renderDiagnosticPretty are the shared
          policy exports.

        - The source-backed structural decode, Result mapping, typed Effect mapping, and retained
          Exit/Cause form is illustrative. Undeclared schemas and compilers remain owned by their
          feature Plans and add no overloads.

        Example:
        ```ts
        const exit = yield * Effect.exit(operation);
        // defect > typed failure > interruption-only
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
         * Source-backed forms (illustrative; undeclared schemas and compilers are
         * owned by their feature Plans and this file adds no overloads):
         *
         * const decoded = decodeStructure(MachineSchema, authored);
         * const compiled = mapResultFailure(decoded, (failure) =>
         *   invalidMachineConfigurationDiagnostic(
         *     "UnexpectedConfigurationField",
         *     ["states", "ACTIVE"],
         *     { field: "debug" },
         *   ),
         * );
         *
         * const load: Effect.Effect<Machine, MachineFailure, RuntimeServices> = ...;
         * const publicLoad = mapEffectFailure(load, diagnosticForMachineFailure);
         *
         * const exit = await Effect.runPromiseExit(publicLoad);
         * if (Exit.isFailure(exit)) {
         *   // retain exit.cause for classification; renderDiagnostic receives only
         *   // the resulting stable Diagnostic projection.
         * }
         */
        ```

    O3  Usage/admission boundary, compatibility, and remediation
        - FlowUsageError is the synchronous JavaScript boundary.
          Its exact declaration is Error with _tag: "FlowUsageError", code:FlowUsageCode,
          path:FlowPath, and details:DiagnosticDetails.
          Its constructor is (code:FlowUsageCode,path?:FlowPath,details?:DiagnosticDetails).
          toFlowUsageError(diagnostic:UsageDiagnostic):FlowUsageError converts only usage/admission
          diagnostics at that boundary.
          Consumers discriminate with _tag and code, then use path/details as stable machine data.
          Inherited Error.message gives human-readable, action-oriented remediation.
          It is not a parsing or control-flow surface.

        - Usage/admission failures use the exact 19 FlowUsageCode values and scalar/null detail
          domain. Raw Cause is not serialized and there is no alternate usage-error shape. Storage,
          codec, identity, and restoration failures remain canonical Diagnostic values internally.
          Serialized diagnostics use private ordered stable Flow diagnostic projections.
          FlowPersistenceError is compatibility-only terminal host envelope when an existing JS
          boundary requires that name; it is never a second expected-error family. Its exact
          declaration is Error with no additional fields. DiagnosticClassification/code continue to
          distinguish Failure/Defect/Interruption.

        - The vNext additive remediation amendment requires one maintained documentation row per
          exact usage code.
          Each row gives failure meaning, relevant path/details, an illustrative human message, and
          corrective action. Messages may evolve; exact prose and
          whitespace are not compatibility assertions. Rich code-linked documentation may explain
          boundaries. It must not expose raw Cause, add a public cause member, or create an
          alternate error shape.
          It must not make message parsing control flow or guarantee message stability.

        - `InvalidCanonicalValue`: A canonical key/value contains an unsupported
          or non-canonical value.
          Path/details: path: ["key"]; details: { "reason": "unsupported" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The operation key contains a value that cannot be canonicalized.
          Corrective action:
            Replace it with a bounded JSON-safe canonical value.
        - `ForeignActorRef`: The ref belongs to another app or runtime.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "reason": "foreign" }
          Message (illustrative):
            This actor ref belongs to a different runtime.
          Corrective action:
            Use a ref created by the receiving app/runtime.
        - `MismatchedActorRef`: The ref is branded for a different machine.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "machine": "Editor" }
          Message (illustrative):
            This actor ref is for a different machine.
          Corrective action:
            Pass the exact machine-branded ref required by the operation.
        - `MissingActorRef`: An operation requires an actor ref that was not supplied.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "required": true }
          Message (illustrative):
            An actor ref is required for this operation.
          Corrective action:
            Provide the admitted stable ref or use the explicit local-actor path.
        - `DisposedActorRef`: A command addressed an actor after terminal disposal.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "disposed": true }
          Message (illustrative):
            This actor has been disposed and cannot accept commands.
          Corrective action:
            Acquire a valid owner/runtime and do not reuse the disposed ref.
        - `RuntimeNotReady`: A handle or command escaped before readiness completed.
          Path/details: path: ["runtime"]; details: { "phase": "booting" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The runtime is still booting.
          Corrective action:
            Await the sole public `runtime.ready()` Effect before use.
        - `RuntimeDisposed`: A command or lookup targeted a disposed runtime.
          Path/details: path: ["runtime"]; details: { "phase": "disposed" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The runtime is disposed and cannot accept work.
          Corrective action:
            Construct and own a new runtime execution scope.
        - `MissingContextProvider`: A declared context binding has no admitted provider.
          Path/details: path: ["contextBindings", "session"]; details: { "provider": "missing" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The required context provider is not admitted.
          Corrective action:
            Admit the exact provider ref before constructing the consumer.
        - `ContextDependencyCycle`: Context bindings contain a dependency cycle.
          Path/details: path: ["contextBindings"]; details: { "cycle": "Session>Theme>Session" }
          Message (illustrative):
            Context bindings contain a dependency cycle.
          Corrective action:
            Remove the cycle and keep provider dependencies acyclic.
        - `DuplicateActorClaim`: Two admissions claim one stable actor identity incompatibly.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "claim": "duplicate" }
          Message (illustrative):
            This stable actor is already claimed incompatibly.
          Corrective action:
            Join the existing ensure or use a distinct stable identity.
        - `UnadmittedMachine`: A machine is not part of the closed `AppPlan`.
          Path/details: path: ["machine"]; details: { "machine": "Editor" }
          Message (illustrative):
            This machine is not admitted by the application plan.
          Corrective action:
            Include the machine in the app before runtime construction.
        - `ActorNotActive`: Work was sent while the actor was not active.
          Path/details: path: ["actor"]; details: { "lifecycle": "suspended" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The actor is not active and cannot accept this work.
          Corrective action:
            Resume through its owning host or wait for runtime activation.
        - `InvalidOperationPlan`: An authored operation plan is structurally invalid.
          Path/details: path: ["operation"]; details: { "reason": "invalid-plan" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The operation plan is invalid.
          Corrective action:
            Fix the descriptor-owned operation fields before admission.
        - `WrongOperationKind`: A resource, transaction, or stream API received another family.
          Path/details: path: ["operation", "kind"]; details: { "expected": "resource" }
          Message (illustrative):
            The operation belongs to a different operation family.
          Corrective action:
            Use the API matching the declaration's exact operation kind.
        - `OperationNotPending`: An action requires a pending operation but none is pending.
          Path/details: path: ["operation"]; details: { "status": "idle" }
          Message (illustrative):
            This operation is not pending.
          Corrective action:
            Start or observe the correct occurrence before acting on it.
        - `OperationAlreadySettled`: A completion/cancellation attempted to
          settle an occurrence twice.
          Path/details: path: ["operation", "occurrence"]; details: { "settled": true }
          Message (illustrative):
            This operation occurrence is already settled.
          Corrective action:
            Do not reuse the settled occurrence; issue a new authored attempt.
        - `DuplicateStreamDeclaration`: One actor declaration installed the same stream slot twice.
          Path/details: path: ["streams", "progress"]; details: { "duplicate": true }
          Message (illustrative):
            This stream declaration is duplicated.
          Corrective action:
            Keep one declaration per slot or give the declarations distinct identities.
        - `BlockedByDependents`: Disposal would violate active or suspended context dependents.
          Path/details: path: ["actorRef"]; details: { "dependents": 1 }
          Message (illustrative):
            This actor cannot be disposed while dependents remain bound.
          Corrective action:
            Dispose or rebind every named dependent before disposal.

        - `AdmissionOverloaded`: New work exceeds a finite active command or serialized-work bound.
          Path/details identify the admission boundary and private capacity without exposing payloads.
          Message (illustrative):
            The runtime cannot admit more work at this boundary yet.
          Corrective action:
            Wait for accepted work to settle before sending new work.
          Preserve already accepted work and FIFO settlement. Reject an entire new finite batch
          before any partial occurrence, preview, or adapter work.

        - Every table row's message is maintained illustration, not a required literal. The stable
          discriminator/fields are _tag, code, path, details. The exact machine-readable
          usage-diagnostic shape is not expanded or replaced by this remediation guidance.
          `OperationAlreadySettled` directs consumers to avoid the settled occurrence and issue a
          new authored attempt.

        - Status: Remediation guidance is a vNext additive amendment. AMEND-API-005 is
          source provenance for the ownerless-name deferral. Preserve both statuses and
          do not infer an active root export.

decisions
    D1  One package-owned diagnostic value
        Diagnostic is the canonical structured failure value and compatibility
        surface; its schema owns codes/fields while summary/help text can change.
        Aliases/projections do not create additional diagnostic models.

    D2  Map typed failures only at owning boundaries
        Structural SchemaError, semantic Result failure, and understood typed
        Effect E map to Diagnostic at their owning boundary. Preserve A/R and
        keep defect/interruption in complete Cause until final classification.
        Convert to FlowUsageError only for usage/admission at synchronous JS.

constraints
    C1  Stable machine data versus human text
        - _tag/code/path/details are machine-readable. summary/help and inherited Error.message are
          not parsable control flow. Do not expose raw Cause in serialized data or renderer input.

    C2  No second error or execution framework
        - Do not add a feature-owned expected Error/Data.TaggedError carrier or new failure family.
          Do not add a diagnostic service, Context.Service, Layer, Logger, custom Effect wrapper,
          or generic runner.
          FlowPersistenceError remains terminal host compatibility only.
          Raw Cause is retained only by the two named host errors in
          @plan.PublicSurface.O1.

    C3  Current root deferral is settled
        - Follow @plan.PublicSurface.O1: FlowPath, FlowUsageCode, FlowUsageError, and
          FlowPersistenceError remain absent from the active root.
          They remain absent until owners and proofs exist.
          Their declarations describe future typed contracts and do not restore current exports.
          @Q1 concerns only future meaning of the existing-boundary wording.

verification
    V1  Canonical model and declaration shape
        - Outcome: @O1.

        - Scenario: compile/decode valid and malformed Diagnostic values and inspect the packed
          declarations.

        - Pass when fields and value domains match @O1 exactly.
          Diagnostic is a Schema.TaggedError, Error, and Effect failure.
          The document type derives from Schema.Codec.Encoded.
          Code and invalid-machine reason sets are closed. DiagnosticProjection adds no model.

        - Check path: nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types; nub run --filter
          flow-state-rewrite check:packed-consumers; nub run test --
          packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/diagnostic/test/diagnostic.test.ts.

        - Pending gap: declarations/runtime proofs must assert every code, classification, Schema
          field, and finite path/detail domain.

    V2  Mapping and Cause-classification behavior
        - Outcome: @O2.

        - Scenario: decode malformed structure, map semantic Result errors, map typed Effect errors,
          and classify mixed Fail/Die/Interrupt Cause.

        - Pass when decoding maps only SchemaError once and returns plain data.
          Result mapping preserves A. Effect mapping preserves A/R.
          Classification precedence is defect, then typed failure, then interruption-only.
          Cause remains complete until cleanup and evidence aggregation finish.
          Renderers receive Diagnostic only; they never receive raw Cause or input.

        - Check path: nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types; nub run test --
          packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/diagnostic/test/diagnostic.test.ts.

        - Pending gap: the production Cause precedence/Exit and host-boundary proof matrix must be
          explicit; typechecks do not prove it.

    V3  Usage codes and remediation coverage
        - Outcome: @O3.

        - Scenario: trigger every usage/admission code and inspect its declaration, path/details,
          documentation row, and representative human message.

        - Pass when exactly the 18 codes in @O1 and @O3 are discriminable by _tag/code.
          Every row gives meaning, path/details, and corrective action.
          Messages are actionable, but exact prose and whitespace are not compatibility assertions.
          No alternate shape, cause field, or raw Cause is exposed.

        - Check path: nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types; nub run test --
          packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/diagnostic/test/diagnostic.test.ts.

        - Pending gap: exhaustive per-code negative/declaration tests and the named exhaustive
          documentation-row proof remain required.

    V4  Deferred-name route and declaration proof
        - Outcomes: @O1, @O3.

        - Scenario: compare active root declarations/exports with @plan.PublicSurface.O1 and
          compare deferred types with @O1 and @O3; ERRORS.ts is source provenance.

        - Pass when FlowPath, FlowUsageCode, FlowUsageError, and FlowPersistenceError remain absent
          from the active root until their owners and proofs exist. Their future declaration shapes
          remain intact, and source declarations are not treated as active root exports.

        - Check path: nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:packed-consumers;
          packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/public/test/public-routes.test.ts.

        - Pending gap: export-absence and declaration-shape proofs remain required. Future
          compatibility meaning of the existing-boundary wording remains open at @Q1.

questions
    Q1  Future meaning of existing-boundary wording
        Current root treatment is settled by @plan.PublicSurface.O1 and the temporary
        ownerless-name amendment.
        FlowPath, FlowUsageCode, FlowUsageError, and FlowPersistenceError are absent until their
        owners and proofs exist.
        Source text calls FlowPath an existing public spelling and FlowUsageError
        an existing synchronous public boundary; ERRORS.ts records their future
        declarations and FlowPersistenceError's compatibility-only terminal host
        envelope. Those files are provenance, not proof of a shipped boundary.
        Before restoring these names, verify product documentation and package history;
        this question does not reopen the current root deferral.

